    solution: "<h1>Verified Certificates on a Shared Ledger</h1><h2>The problem</h2><p>Employers cannot quickly check whether a degree or training certificate is genuine, and fake certificates are common. Checking by letter takes weeks.</p><h2>Who is involved</h2><p>Colleges issue certificates, students hold them, and employers and other colleges need to verify them.</p><h2>How a blockchain would work</h2><p>Each college would publish a hash of every certificate, a fingerprint of its content, in a block on a shared ledger kept by the participating colleges. Consensus among the colleges decides which blocks are added, and nobody can quietly change an old entry without breaking the chain. An employer would hash the certificate they receive and compare it with the record.</p><h2>Is it justified</h2><p>Partly. Several institutions that do not fully trust each other benefit from a shared tamper-evident record, but a single government-run database could also work. The blockchain only stores fingerprints, not the personal data, which protects privacy.</p><h2>Risks</h2><ul><li>Colleges could issue fake entries, so an issuer must be approved and identified.</li><li>Students could lose their access, so recovery must be planned.</li><li>Rules on privacy and data deletion must be respected.</li></ul><h2>Next steps</h2><ol><li>Interview five employers and two colleges about how they verify today.</li><li>Build a simple prototype that records the hash of ten certificates.</li><li>Test it with a pilot college and measure how long a check takes.</li></ol>",
